just in the last week or so there's been  a couple of interesting stories from  space both involving water in space in  one way or another so we thought we have  a quick chat and tell you about those so  the first one is is that it's the  classic water story in some ways which  is water on Mars it's something that  people have been fascinated about for  centuries I guess when people first  thought there might be canals on Mars  then that introduced the idea there  might be water on Mars and the  fascination obviously fundamentally  arises from the fact that we think that  water is sort of fundamentally  associated with life and there's the  best evidence today of liquid water on  the surface of Mars has just been  released this is a space probe out there  at the moment the Mars Reconnaissance  Orbiter NASA probe which has been taking  pictures of the surface of Mars for some  years now Martian years now and one of  the things that they've recently  discovered is that on some hillsides on  Mars in winter there's nothing much  going on and then in the Martian spring  you start finding these little sort of  dark streaks appearing on these  hillsides and as you follow them through  the summer months you find the streaks  get longer and longer and sometimes they  bifurcate they split into two sometimes  they merge together and for all the  world they look like little trickles of  water running down the hillside this is  molten arrow that you can see running on  the surface of Mars which means that you  know when it's it's not there on the  surface it must be very close to the  surface and so the idea now is that if  you're finding this this much water on  the surface if you could actually dig  down the bit then potentially there  could be great reservoirs of the stuff  down there there is a one interesting  issue which is if you kind of figure out  what the surface temperature and  atmospheric pressure and so on on Mars  is likely to be they shouldn't really be  any liquid water on the surface it  should still be frozen it's very cold  still and so the only way you can  actually make it that there is actually  liquid water on the surface of Mars is  if it isn't pure water if it has to be  brine it has to be very salty water  because if you add salt to water it  lowers the freezing temperature  considerably and although salty water is  not very conducive to life there are  actually quite a lot of bacteria here on  earth that really thrive in a salty  environment and so the idea is that if  you're really looking for life on Mars  the best bet at the moment is that  they're likely to be these sort of salt  loving bacteria living in these trickles  of water how come we're never catching  the smoking gun it just seems too weird  that we never actually see it we always  just missed it by  few hours I think it's at least in part  that people are very conservative about  where they land things because you want  to make sure when you land a probe on  Mars that it's not going to tip over  when it lands that it's not going to  fall down a ravine and so on so people  tend to pick fairly dull places to land  and not surprisingly the exciting stuff  like running water and so on tends to  happen in the hilly places and the more  exciting places so as we get a bit more  adventurous or start building probes  that can actually travel larger  distances on Mars I think we will  actually find these things but I it is  just this effect that you tend to be a  bit conservative where you put them down  which means that you tend to pick the  dull places so not surprisingly the  place turns out looking a little bit  dull as well and how come whenever they  see evidence of liquid flowing they  always say it's water couldn't be other  liquids that cause these little rivets  and erosion on the sides of hills it  could be they're all that many materials  that are actually liquid at those kind  of temperatures so there aren't a huge  number of things to choose from and  because both hydrogen and oxygen are  very abundant in the universe the most  likely thing that you're going to find  running on the service is going to be  water it really is odds on that they  have found water now I don't think it's  not one of those things that someone  who's going to come up with an  alternative explanation I suspect quite  how important it is for you know life on  Mars and those kinds of things remains  to be seen but now it's interesting  story soon  it is related to water but actually let  me start with oxygen oxygen is a very  abundant element in the universe one of  the most abundant there is and yet  bizarrely astronomers have never  actually found molecular oxygen anywhere  in the universe we can find it in other  compounds carbon monoxide for example  exists a lot that's carbon and oxygen  together but oxygen on its own Oh two  oxygen plus oxygen has never been found  before except here on earth yes and I've  out there in space I meant to say yes  and so people have tried to come up with  explanations as to why they can't find  it and that's where we come back to  water again  because the authorities that probably a  lot of the oxygen out there is actually  locked up in water and then you can't  then see lots and lots of water because  that water is then frozen as ice and if  you take those take that ice and put it  on the surface of little grains in space  so you have these little sort of sand  grains and dust grains that are cut or  covered in in little icy coats then  they're very chemically nonreactive and  so actually you don't see very much  going on and you're not going to produce  molecular oxygen from those so someone  come up with a clever experiment to  actually try and and detect it for the  first time which is if this story is  right and all that water is out there so  all the oxygen is locked up in water on  these dust grains if you look at  somewhere where those little icy coats  are being melted away and so it's turned  back into liquid water then when you've  got liquid water you have much more  chance of chemical reactions then so you  can then convert some of that oxygen  from the water into molecular oxygen  there's a chance to detect it so what  the these guys are done with the  Herschel Space Telescope the ISA mission  that monitors the universe in the  infrared is looked at some star forming  regions for the signature of oxygen of  course in a star forming region you've  got lots of new stars forming which are  heating up their environments which  means if there are any of these little  frozen grains out there the water will  get melted on the surface of them so the  hope was well if we look at these star  forming regions we'll actually see that  water melting away and then some of it  being converted into oxygen so we can  actually do take the oxygen and sure  enough when they actually did the  experiment they found for the first time  the clear signature of oxygen in the  data that they were taking so you have  to do spectroscopy now so you have to  split the light up into a spectrum and  then because the oxygen molecules absorb  particular wavelengths of light and  re-emit particular wavelengths of light  you can find those signatures in the  spectral that very particular  wavelengths of light from the spectrum  might be missing entirely or you might  have an extra excess at a particular  wavelength even though these are what  for astronomers are quite dense regions  because the stars forming there it's  still comparable to the best vacuums you  can get on earth so no there's tiny  amount of oxygen you certainly wouldn't  want to be out there trying to breathe  it

